arrival is a classically crafted slice  of sci-fi with a sublime performance by  Amy Adams at its core but how did the  filmmakers actually put the film  together in this video I'm going to look  at seven fascinating secrets you need to  know about the making of the movie the  Hebda pods spaceship owes its design to  an asteroid named after the Greek  goddess of Lauren legislation eunomia  during research for the design of the  alien crafts director denis villeneuve  came across the asteroid 15 eunomia was  attracted by what he called it's insane  shape like a strange egg and thought  that kind of pebble or oval shape would  bring a perfect sense of menace and  mystery to the hepta pod spacecraft  which in the film is made from materials  unknown to man and defies current human  scientific understanding in terms of how  it works and travels through space  octopuses whales elephants and even  spiders were all sources of inspiration  when it came to creating the aliens  Abbott and Costello Filner who wanted  their design to evoke a very strong  presence an air of intelligence and the  feeling of being close to a huge beast  underwater and he wanted to bring to  life the sort of surreal creature you  might imagine in a dream or nightmare  and in the later stages of the film he  wanted them to be suggestive of the Grim  Reaper on top of that it was important  to Vil nerve that the reveal of the full  alien body happened slowly over the  course of the film in order to build the  audience's suspense and intrigue and he  also wanted to contrast their quiet  arrival and calm communication with  Louise and Ian inside the ship with the  panicked reaction of the world outside  to achieve this he brought onboard  creature concept artist Carlos  one--there who worked on Ridley Scott's  Prometheus the underwater creatures like  octopuses and squid which so influenced  the hepta pods appearance also inspired  the filmmakers to use blasts of ink to  form their language while the idea that  the hepta pods nonlinear language should  consist of circles marked with twists  and curls came from screenwriter Eric  Heisler who even included rough sketches  of them in his script  the aliens language is an absolutely  crucial part of the film and Vilna have  wanted it to be striking almost  frightening completely distinct from  human language and suggestive of a  different mind set so high Sora's  concept was further developed by  production designer  trees ferment working with artist  Martine Bertrand whose idea it was for  the language to appear as abstract in  key circles that resembled coffee stains  the Mets and his team then immerse  themselves in the design process of  creating a fictional language that had  its own set of rules and was internally  accurate not just a randomly assembled  bunch of circles splotches and  flourishes in fact they created their  own dictionary with over a hundred  logograms  each of which conveys full sentences or  paragraphs and 71 of those were used in  the film dirty sci-fi is what Vil nerve  and his cinematographer Bradford Jung  called the look they created together  for a rival basically they wanted to  avoid the kind of spectacle you see in a  typical sci-fi blockbuster and instead  go for something more delicate and light  what that means for the film is that to  quote Bill nerve it feels like this was  happening on a bad Tuesday morning like  when you were a kid on the school bus on  a rainy day and you dream while looking  out the window at the clouds that kind  of atmosphere and a major influence on a  rivals very raw and naturalistic look  for cinematographer Bradford Young was  the work of Scandinavian photographer  Martina Hoagland even of especially the  subdued and mysterious quality of her  exhibition and book Speedway the film  arrival that we see today is very  different than it was originally going  to be as its whole structure was changed  in post-production  according to ville nerve while the  original screenplay made sense on paper  the visual medium of cinema made telling  a nonlinear story with a twist like a  rival much more challenging and when the  director got into the editing room he  realized to be told to film exactly as  it wasn't the script its structure  wouldn't be dynamic enough it would feel  repetitive in places and there would be  some discrepancies within the characters  fortunately Arrivals editor Joe Walker  came to the rescue and suggested  restructuring the footage they'd shot in  a less linear fashion which also meant  that the very structure of the film  would reflect some of the movie's main  themes one of the only Hollywood studios  that showed any interest in making a  rival when the script was initially  pitched to them actually wanted a male  protagonist to replace the film central  character linguist dr. Louise Banks who  by the way is also the lead in the short  story the films based on however the  movies producers stuck to their guns and  the film independently and later with  Amy Adams attached they sold the rights  to distribute the film in North America  to Paramount for a record sum of twenty  million dollars at Cannes Film Festival  in 2014 interestingly enough before the  role of dr. banks an arrival came along  Adams had been planning to take some  time away from making movies and just  hang out with her family however when  she read the screenplay she fell in love  with the story and the opportunity to  work with denis villeneuve was also too  much for draw to turn down originally  the film was named after Ted Chang short  story but directed an evil nerve decided  to change the film's title from story of  your life because he felt that audiences  would think the film was a rom-com  rather than a sci-fi movie and because  he believed they'd moved away from the  short stories focus indeed to increase  the film's drama and tension the  screenplay made some significant changes  to the original tale for example the  script added global conflict by having  the aliens arrive in a dozen locations  on earth which in turn kicked off a  ticking clock scenario and in the end  after considering hundreds of potential  titles the filmmakers actually settled  on the very first one they thought of  arrival now I'd love to know your  favorite moment in arrival and how you  think the film compares with your  favorite sci-fi movies let me know your  thoughts in the comments below plus if  you'd like to talk about the film in  more detail I've got a full spoilery  analysis in my arrival explained video  which you can check out and the link to  the left and if you're a sci-fi fan you  might also like my interstellar video  with 15 cool facts about the making of  that movie you can watch that by  clicking over there on the right if you  enjoyed this video do please like share  and subscribe thanks for watching and  see you next time  yippee-ki-yay movie lovers
 

